[Wand Identification]

Whenever you find a wand, first Engrave something with your finger.
Then Engrave "Elbereth" with the wand.  Do this in a place where you'd
like to have a permanent Elbereth square; perhaps a closet or someplace
near an altar where you'll be likely to do a stash later.  Be prepared
to be blinded by this action.  If the wand is fire or lightning, you
will be given a valuable gift of a permanent safe square.  Much less
permanent safety for a wand of digging, but still a valuable use of the
wasted charge.  Wand of light will light up the room.  I'm not O.C.D. 
enough to make sure this process happens in a dark room, but I suppose
you could do that.  

Be ready to run or fight if you happen to have found "create monster".

Any time you think you've identified a wand, #name it!  I also like to
name the wand according to its material, in hopes that it might somehow 
save me later in a situation with shock or striking, and so on.   (Well
it never actually saves me from carelessness, but there's always the 
chance!)

	Name an individual object? [ynq] (q) y
	What do you want to name? [a-o or ?*] o
	What do you want to name this glass wand? (glass) wand of digging

If you happen to have found a wand of wishing, hopefully you know what
to wish for.  There are ways to maximize your yield of wishes from any
given wand of wishing.

Some wands tell you about the bugs on the floor.  If the bugs stop
moving, name that wand "Sleep or Death".  Maybe you should go find 
out which it is, because it can be a nasty surprise if you think you 
have zapped a wand of death at a sleep resistant monster...  On the 
other hand, against monsters who are not sleep resistant, a wand of 
sleep is almost as good as a wand of death...

If the bugs speed up, name the wand "haste", then zap yourself and your
pet with it.  If they slow down, go hunt your first unicorn with it,
and remember to also use it when you meet your first soldier ant or 
your first leocrotta.

Ice cubes?  Name the wand "frost".  Remember that you can use it to
cross water, perhaps on your quest, on a swamp level, at Medusa, or at
the castle.  Does the wand unsuccessfully fight your attempt to write?
Name it "striking" and *don't* zap it at nymphs, anything carrying
potions, or anything that might be carrying or containing something
like a glass wand of wishing, which refers to the incident that led to
me becoming meticulous about wand identification.  Also, don't assume
that death by exploding drawbridges is something that happens to other
people...

Bullet holes?  Magic missile.  

Wand of polymorph will be obvious -- your Elbereth writing will be
turned into a rumor, perhaps even written in blood.  As with a wand of 
wishing, you've probably anticipated a wand of polymorph and already 
know what you'd like to do with it.

Wand of enlightenment will tell you about yourself.

If the writing vanishes, you've got a bit more work to do.  Name the
wand "C/I/T" because it might be cancellation, invisibility, or
teleport.  DO NOT put it into a bag.  I don't care that your bag isn't a
bag of holding.  Don't put that wand in there.  If you don't happen to
find your writing someplace else on the level, one idea is to go find 
a floating eye, and zap this C/I/T wand at him.  Three possibilities,
well, four actually:  1.  The eye will be teleported somewhere else,
and you can #name the wand "teleport",  2.  The eye will be cancelled, 
and you can attack it, and name that wand "cancellation", and definitely
don't put it in a bag.  (I get nervous putting them into *chests!*)  
3.  The eye will be made invisible, and likewise you can attack it.  
4.  You used the last charge on the engraving, which is pretty unlikely.

Cancellation is a very good weapon against creatures with special
attacks; don't be reluctant to use it!  

What about the wands that give you no message at all?  I tend not to
worry about them until later, and then I setup this procedure:

Find a door, not a shop door, and nowhere near a shop,  and a large
box or a chest.  Place an object into the chest.  Lock the chest. 
unlock the door.  On top, place a lichen corpse.  Step away from the 
chest, and zap the wand in the direction of the chest and door.  
This way, you can identify Locking, Opening, Probing, and Undead Turning.

That leaves a wand of "nothing", and a wand of secret door detection. 
Distinguishing between those two is rather simple, and hardly a priority 
this early in the game.  Wands of nothing often have a good number of 
charges, and they can be handy when polymorphed into some other wand.
My characters are generally very good at detecting doors and traps, 
so wands of secret door detection aren't considered interesting.  Your
mileage may vary.

Obviously, this process becomes simpler each time you identify a wand.  
For instance, once you know "make invisible" and "teleport", you know 
when you have found "cancellation."  And some of these will be
identified for you when creatures use them against you.

Likewise, if all the wands that are id'd by engraving have been found, 
you can avoid wasting a charge on the next one you find.  

I hope this advice helps you to identify your wands with a minimum of 
wasted charges.


